# The act of redressing; a making [[right]]; [[amendment]]; [[correction]]; [[reformation]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:William Shakespeare|chapter=[[w:Henry IV, Part 2|The Second Part of Henry the Fourth, Containing His Death: And the Coronation of King Henry the Fift<!--Not an error.-->]]|title=[[w:First Folio|Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies: Published According to the True Originall Copies]]|location=London|publisher=Printed by [[w:William Jaggard|Isaac Iaggard]], and [[w:Edward Blount|Ed[ward] Blount]]|year=''c.'' 1596–1599|year_published=1623|section=act IV, scene ii|page=91, column 1|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=uNtBAQAAMAAJ&pg=RA1-PA57|oclc=606515358|passage=[[w:John of Lancaster, 1st Duke of Bedford|''Iohn.'' [''of Lancaster'']]] {{...}} Let's drinke together friendly, and embrace, / That all their eyes may beare thoſe Tokens home, / Of our reſtored Loue and Amitie. / [[w:Richard le Scrope|''Biſh.'' [''Archbishop of York'']]] I take your Princely word, for theſe '''redreſſes'''.}}
# A setting right, as of [[injury]], [[oppression]], or [[wrong]], such as the redress of [[grievances]]; ''hence'', [[indemnification]]; [[relief]]; [[remedy]]; [[reparation]].
#: {{syn|en|amends|indemnification|reparation|Thesaurus:compensation}}
#* '''1791''' December 15 (adoption), [[First Amendment]] of the {{w|United States Constitution}}:
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|volume=I|chapter=XVI|page=181|passage="Good heavens!" cried Elizabeth; "but how could ''that'' be?—How could his will be disregarded?—Why did you not seek legal '''redress'''?"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Law Commission (England and Wales)|The Law Commission]]; [[w:Scottish Law Commission|The Scottish Law Commission]]|chapter=The Structure of this Consultation Paper|title=Consumer Redress for Misleading and Aggressive Practices: A Joint Consultation Paper|url=http://www.lawcom.gov.uk/wp-content/uploads/2015/03/cp199_consumer_redress.pdf|series=Law Commission Consultation Paper|seriesvolume=no. 199; Scottish Law Commission Discussion Paper; no. 149|location=London|publisher={{w|The Stationery Office}} on behalf of the Controller of [[w:Office of Public Sector Information|Her Majesty's Stationery Office]]|year=2011|page=24, paragraph 1.25(1)|isbn=978-0-11-840510-2|passage=Although the main sanction is a criminal prosecution, there is also the possibility of consumer '''redress''', either through compensation orders or the new civil sanction pilots.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20151004131459/http://www.lawcom.gov.uk/wp-content/uploads/2015/03/cp199_consumer_redress.pdf|archivedate=4 October 2015}}
# A [[possibility]] to [[set right]], or a possibility to seek a [[remedy]], for instance in [[court]]
# One who, or that which, gives relief; a [[redresser]].

